/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Light is measured in four linked units.
   - On the road, illuminance falls with the square of distance from the lamp:
   - E is the illuminance in lux on a surface facing the lamp, I the intensity of the beam in that direction in candela, and d the distance in metres.
   - Spreading the same flux over a wider beam lowers the intensity in each direction, so a lamp with more lumens does not always light the road better.
   - Incandescence: current heats a tungsten filament until it glows, about 2700 K in a vacuum bulb, giving 10–15 lm/W.
   - Gas discharge: a high-voltage pulse, about 20 kV from the ballast, strikes an arc through xenon gas between two electrodes; metal salts then vaporise and give most of the light.
   - Car lamps went from acetylene and oil lamps to vacuum filament bulbs, then to two-filament bulbs giving main and dipped beam from one lamp.
   - The technical demands on a headlamp are: enough light far down the road; a dipped beam with a sharp cut-off so no light rises into oncoming eyes; fixed colours; resistance to vibration, heat and water; long life; and type approval of the la
   - A source is shaped into a beam by a lamp unit.
*/
