/* book exam traps: Treating a 12 V filament lamp as a constant-power load at other voltages. Its current rises with voltage, but less than in proportion; an estimate with the cold-rated resistance at 13.8 V errs on the high side, which is the safe side for a fuse. | Forgetting the stop lamps: two 21 W lamps add 42 W (3.2 A at 13.2 V) only while the brake pedal is pressed. | Swapping main and dipped beam: only the dipped beam has the asymmetric cut-off. | Breaking the colour rules: white or selective yellow at the front, red at the rear, amber only for indicators, white for reversing and number-plate lamps. | Treating the flasher unit as a fuse. It sets the flash rate, about 90 a minute; with an electronic flasher a faster flash means a lamp has failed. */
