/* book exam traps: Levelling corrects pitch (up and down) for load; swivel corrects yaw (left and right) for steering. A car can have one without the other. | Using the distance from the rear axle to the lamp in the pitch angle. The body turns about the front axle, so use the wheelbase; the lamp's position does not change the angle. | Getting the direction wrong: a sinking rear tilts the beam up, so the motor tilts the lamp down. | Check which angle a swivel gain is defined on. If it is given per degree of road-wheel angle, divide the steering-wheel angle by the steering ratio first. | Ignoring the cap: beyond about 15° more steering gives no more swivel. */
